# The one-part fusions whose conversion is stated, beside the part and the figure filed against
# it. One row per such fusion per quantity. This is the population the identity test applies to,
# before the fusions that eliminate something, or that owe no sum, are taken out.
#
# Why it exists: a difference needs a named left operand
#   |A ∖ B| = |A| - |A ⋉ B| is checkable only where somebody can count A, so a difference taken
#   against a candidate set spelled inline cannot carry a law however plainly it is written. That
#   is the second way a difference goes ungoverned, and examples/soundness/main.rs looks for the
#   first: it walks the tree for difference syntax, and an unnamed operand is invisible to it.
#
# The restrictions here come before the difference
#   The two restrictions here are not the difference and belong on this side of it. A stated
#   factor and a single part are what make identity askable at all; a filed elimination and a
#   suspension are answers to it. Folding all four into one `WHERE` is what hid the operator.
#
# The conversion is the same one rule
#   The part is converted at the corner its sign picks, which is what composition/converted.sqlc
#   does on the route the identity test compares this population against. Two expressions for one
#   operator would have this population disagree with the figure it is the population for, on any
#   filing whose quantity went negative. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `two_slopes`.
#
# One part filed, not one part resolved
#   The single part is counted by folds/fusion_parts.sqlc, over the parts the composer filed. A
#   fusion filing two parts, one naming a document this corpus does not hold, is not a one-part
#   fusion, and counting only the parts that resolve here would test it as though it were.
-- composition/part_quantities.sqlc for a fusion that files one part, where the factor has a figure.
SELECT q.composition AS filing, q.composed_layer AS layer, q.quantity,
       least(   q.part_low  * coalesce(q.factor_low, 1),
                q.part_low  * coalesce(q.factor_high, 1)) AS part_low,
       q.part_mode * coalesce(q.factor_mode, 1)           AS part_mode,
       greatest(q.part_high * coalesce(q.factor_low, 1),
                q.part_high * coalesce(q.factor_high, 1)) AS part_high,
       q.part_unit,
       q.composed_low AS filed_low, q.composed_mode AS filed_mode, q.composed_high AS filed_high,
       q.composed_unit AS filed_unit
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/part_quantities.sqlc)
) q
JOIN      (
    :compose(folds/fusion_parts.sqlc)
) f ON f.composition = q.composition AND f.composed_layer = q.composed_layer
WHERE q.factor_state IN ('omitted', 'stated')
  AND f.parts = 1
